In the given text, Parent is discussing a section on "Distances measures with constraints" which includes topics like anchored distances and speed-constrained distances. This section introduces two types of distance measures that incorporate additional conditions or constraints to the traditional discrete Fréchet and DTW (Dynamic Time Warping) distances:

1. Anchored distances, where a traversal is considered w-anchored if each vertex is paired with another vertex at a maximum distance of w/2 from it on both curves. The width parameter 'w' determines the maximum allowed distance between vertices in the traversal and must be even for simplicity.

2. Speed-constrained distances, which introduces two more types of constrained distances: anchored distances with an anchor constraint (anchored discrete Fréchet and DTW distances) and speed-constrained distances that limit the degree of the bipartite graph representing the traversal to at most 'w' vertices on each side.

The parent text also discusses a new family H' of hashing schemes, constructed by concatenating k= max {1,log α2(1/n)} hash functions from an original family H of hash functions. This increases the collision probability and query time but reduces memory requirements when 𝛼2 = 0.

The parent text also mentions that traditional hashing can be used to shorten long hash values for more efficient searches in LSH (Locality-Sensitive Hashing) tables, which would increase α2 by an additive O(1/n) term.
